nt","note":"Each quarter, central banks and other holders of official reserves tell the IMF, in confidence, which currency their foreign exchange reserves are held in, and the IMF publishes only the world total, so no country appears here. A currency is what a reserve asset is denominated in, not who issued it: a dollar bond from a Japanese bank counts as dollars. Reserves are valued at each quarter's exchange rates, so a fall in euro reserves can be a fall in the euro rather than a sale. The Australian and Canadian dollars are counted on their own from the end of 2012 and the yuan from the end of 2016; before that they sit in other currencies. From the third quarter of 2025 the IMF estimates the currency of reserves some holders do not report, and the row under the total shows how much that is. Gold, special drawing rights and the reserve position in the IMF are not foreign exchange reserves; official gold has its own book. Source: International Monetary Fund, Currency Composition of Official Foreign Exchange Reserves (COFER); shares and the estimated amount are our calculations."}